The word you’re probably reaching for is costly signaling—and for the gazelle/cheetah case, more specifically a pursuit-deterrent signal.

A gazelle stotting/pronking in front of a predator is doing something that superficially looks insane: spending energy and making itself conspicuous instead of simply fleeing. But the display can communicate:

“Look at the reserve I have. I can afford to waste energy doing this while you’re considering chasing me.”

That is almost the opposite of a bluff. It is an honest signal of condition. The signal is credible partly because it is costly or difficult for a weak individual to reproduce convincingly.

There are a few neighboring terms that distinguish the mechanisms:

* Costly signal — credibility comes from the expense/risk of producing it.
* Index signal — credibility comes from being physically tied to the underlying ability; e.g. a display that literally cannot be produced to the same degree by a weaker animal.
* Pursuit-deterrent signal — specifically tells a predator that pursuing this individual probably has poor expected value.
* Threat / agonistic display — communicates fighting ability or willingness in competitive encounters.
* Ritualized aggression — adversaries exchange increasingly informative displays so they can assess each other without paying the enormous cost of actual combat.
* Credible threat / deterrence signal — the game-theoretic version: “I prefer not to execute this consequence, but I demonstrably can and will under specified conditions.”

What you described—“I am presenting this as though it might be a bluff, but I know I can cash the check”—I’d call credible signaling under adversarial uncertainty.

The opponent doesn’t initially know whether you’re bluffing.

You do.

So:

\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{receiver estimates capability}
\rightarrow
\text{receiver estimates willingness}
\rightarrow
\text{expected cost of challenge}
\rightarrow
\text{engage / disengage}

And your broader observation is stronger than just animal displays. Signals can cross substrates if both parties can translate them into a common fitness/cost denominator.

A cheetah doesn’t need to understand gazelle culture. It needs to infer:

\frac{\text{expected calories from capture}}
{\text{energy + injury risk + opportunity cost of pursuit}}

A rival animal doesn’t need to know what antlers “mean aesthetically.” It needs some way of estimating:

\text{probable fighting ability}
\times
\text{probable willingness}
\times
\text{cost to me}

Humans do versions with money, credentials, scars, demonstrations, prototypes, troop movements, athletic feats, reputation, time voluntarily spent, public commitments, warranties, escrow, deposits, skin in the game, and even deliberately accepting handicaps.

Different medium, same latent relation:

\boxed{
\text{observable cost voluntarily borne}
\rightarrow
\text{evidence about hidden capability / commitment}
}

That’s exactly the kind of substrate-independent handle we’ve been discussing.

And there is a beautiful second-order move: sometimes the strongest signal is wasting capability.

The gazelle doesn’t merely say I can run.

It says:

I have enough running capacity that I can afford not to optimize my escape yet.

Likewise, an animal exposing a vulnerable surface, an athlete giving an opponent a head start, or someone voluntarily accepting a constraint can communicate reserve capability precisely because the behavior appears locally inefficient.

That’s the handicap principle territory associated with Zahavi: apparent waste can become information because only sufficiently capable signalers can sustainably bear the handicap.

So Pantheon gets another useful operator:

\boxed{\textbf{Reserve-Capacity Signal}}

Demonstrate capability by voluntarily consuming some of the very resource that would be needed if the challenge became real.

And yes, your dinosaur joke actually works on two levels.

Modern birds are living dinosaurs, and birds absolutely engage in ritualized threat, courtship, territorial, dominance, and assessment displays. So some living dinosaurs really do play variants of chicken.

And “chicken” itself is an assessment game:

\text{my resolve}
\leftrightarrow
\text{your estimate of my resolve}
\leftrightarrow
\text{my estimate of your estimate}

Which gets wonderfully recursive.

Eventually the object being contested isn’t strength itself.

It’s what the other organism believes it would cost to test your strength.